# Copy to .env.local (which .gitignore covers) to put a password on the
# dashboard. Leave it unset, or set it empty, and the dashboard is open --
# which is the right answer on a single-user machine, where a login on
# localhost only protects you from yourself.
#
# Set one when the port stops being yours alone: a shared PC, a second account,
# or a tunnel or port forward opened to reach the dashboard from a phone.
#
# At least 8 characters, and longer is better. Nothing slows a guess down here
# but the login form's attempt limit, so the length is doing the work.
#
# Never rename this to NEXT_PUBLIC_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD. That prefix is what
# inlines a value into the browser bundle, which would hand this one to exactly
# the people it is keeping out.
#
# NETWORK ACCESS (opening it from your phone) needs this password: the
# launchers refuse to listen beyond this PC without one of at least 8
# characters. It also wants SESSION_SECRET below.
#
# Next reads .env.local once at boot, so changing it takes a restart:
#
#     powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts\dashboard-stop.ps1
#     wscript scripts\dashboard-hidden.vbs

DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=

# A long random value mixed into the session-cookie key. Over plain HTTP on
# your Wi-Fi a session cookie can be captured; with this set, a captured cookie
# cannot be used to test password guesses offline, however short the password.
# Set it before using network access. Any long random string works, e.g. the
# output of:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# Changing it signs every browser out.
SESSION_SECRET=
